9 #edb/ref 17592186428726 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050991 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428727 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050991 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428728 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050991 #edb/ref 1031 "The Intersection of Two Planes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050991 #edb/ref 1033 2539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050991 #edb/ref 1034 1.179829750799535 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050991 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186146926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146924 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c5616580-763b-5332-8f52-a3f7d2017eaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146926 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨2,-1,-5⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c64926a1-902a-58d9-ba27-0fd710e7dd4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146929 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨1,-2,-3⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146929 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186146929 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d21c6b9-3f99-5745-ab7b-e3a41222131c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147341 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147341 #edb/ref 1071 "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147341 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147341 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147342 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147342 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5 &=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186147342 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147342 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147343 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147343 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nIII. The planes intersect on a straight line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147343 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186148200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147425 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147341 #edb/ref 175921