50991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147718 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147718 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9b33318d-1bab-5993-91f6-d6c8c1d3d0a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148195 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148195 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148195 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5=0 \\\\ 2x-y+z+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148195 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4dd6df0-763b-5991-88eb-0cc2466040ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148195 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148200 #edb/ref 1071 "The intersection of the planes $-x+y-2z=0$ and $-x-2y+z-3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c01a5d8f-f2ce-5a5d-bfd0-e648dd7fcc1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148200 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148276 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148276 #edb/ref 1061 21910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148276 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ae4dc56-971c-585e-ac6e-a1d1927975cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148276 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148277 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148277 #edb/ref 1061 21917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148277 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27bcbb90-63f9-5635-829b-a801f3809b3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148277 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148278 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148278 #edb/ref 1061 21951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ba22f44-3353-5e3e-a64c-1e09fd59d6d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148279 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148279 #edb/ref 1061 21954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148279 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4a1265b-c056-54ef-b3c5-eaaf48e49ec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148279 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148280 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148280 #edb/ref 1061 21955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148280 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cba5c95d-7e83-5872-ae95-b6832ff486a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148280 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148281 #edb/ref 1061 22145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8aec20bb-3034-54e2-b58d-43a8fc04ec0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148743 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148743 #edb/ref 1065 3803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148743 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e276ddb-1c14-51fd-8261-8c02eb3294f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148743 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148744 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, given two planes \n\n$$\n{\\Pi_{{1}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{1}x+b_{1}y + c_{1}z+ d_1=0 \\qquad  \\text{and} \\qquad   {\\Pi_{{2}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{2}x+b_{2}y + c_{2}z+ d_2=0,\n$$\n\n we have the following cases, regarding the positions of their normal vectors  $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2 \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- *Case 1*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\nparallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ then ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$   and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ intersect on a straight line.\n\n- *Case 2*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\parallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ but the corresponding equations of the planes are not scalar multiples of each other, then the planes are distinct and are parallel to each other.\n\n- *Case 3*:  If the corresponding coefficients of ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ are all proportional, then the planes are coincident." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148744 #edb/ref 1065 3804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148744 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "50ffbdb5-c9d0-5815-9b29-b505cde7f0fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148744 #edb/ref 1067 "Intersection of Two Planes in 3D" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148745 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n![](images/ca/ca43fd13bd65c544d66a4de2801871c2a79087fd960db44696ce6068d30c8377.svg)\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n![](images/1a/1a97b5bcff75bc7306d14a8af3fb1f169a2e7bf865be2e7a9c6d2741eecc63a6.svg)\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n![](images/1f/1f4cce850809f3fda876dd96e1cf9c65fd873df97bbba44e291b611896c86ca4.svg)\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148745 #edb/ref 1065 3817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148745 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7303bab-ef2c-5a9b-9caa-dc6fcc7929d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148745 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cartesian Equation of a Line as the Intersection of Two Planes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260264 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260264 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260264 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2bbb0f75-5837-5fef-9156-1bb0626a6799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260355 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "358aeb92-2af7-5b63-a3fc-98a16739711a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260517 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ae1bb4f-6418-5cbe-8748-f187b44dbec8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261019 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "83b18a92-b695-5c83-b721-b2dee329f376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261817 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261817 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261817 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db99d772-027a-5c17-b647-51f20577ed39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186260517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262235 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3e5261fb-a30e-5c21-b272-d448a6432184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449206 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449207 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449208 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n\n- **II.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d75bf5e3-9255-56f7-a884-2df425d0b593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449212 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186265703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449213 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186265703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449214 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186265703 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265703 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265703 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $3x+y-2z=0$ and $2x+y+3z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$\n- **II.** The planes are parallel.\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186265703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "999cfdc7-ea79-5a7b-bd15-dce6f8b9e282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265703 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y+3z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i-13j+k \\\\  & =⟨5,-13,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only I and III only are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336049 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨-1,1,-3⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336049 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336049 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0211111d-fe45-5a09-9006-78c755f45403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336103 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨4,-5,0⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336103 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336103 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "086f5368-e828-52b2-91a9-64b0973c30d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336182 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,0,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336182 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336182 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1030568a-af15-5382-9a1b-bf9c88339fda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336274 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,1,-5⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336274 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336274 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a55379d-1dd0-5770-a7d1-c7f2cb774197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336345 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨20,25,20⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336345 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336345 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21a0f372-608b-5fee-a050-b16d2e06eaf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336482 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-5,\\,b=⟨1,0,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336482 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336482 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2eaa47b7-38f8-58da-9f30-aeb4369d4b27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336523 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨-1,0,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336523 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336523 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3442db29-a582-52f1-a4b5-cba4c9870b09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336674 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨5,-4,0⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218633667