8477 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186336345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338477 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "74b18175-4604-545f-86e0-7cd07ba8bb55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338923 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338923 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338923 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338923 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+z-3 &=0 \\\\ 3x+2y-5 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186338923 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bb7afc4-6665-54d6-aaac-0d0fbaf7ddf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338923 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩\\times ⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338926 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-4y &=0 \\\\ 4y-5z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186338926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0178351-a186-5d55-9077-57136241af31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338926 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-4y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,-4,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y-5z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-5⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-5⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =20i+25j+20k \\\\  & =⟨20,25,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338944 #edb/ref 1071 "The intersection of the planes $x+2y-4z+6=0$ and $x-4y+2z=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fd28fdb-4130-5881-857a-aa4f88117872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338944 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n0-4y+2z=0\\,⟹\\,z=2y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $z=2y$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y-4z+6 & =0 \\\\ 0+2y-4(2y)+6 & =0 \\\\ -6y & =-6 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $z=2y=2$ and $a=⟨0,1,2⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-4z+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,-4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-4y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-4,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,-4⟩\\times ⟨1,-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i-6j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-12,-6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{6}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨2,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,2⟩+t⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1+2=3,$ and $b=⟨2,1,1⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452604 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186338946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452605 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186338946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452606 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186338946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338946 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $x-3y+z=0$ and $2x-y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,5,2⟩.$\n- **II.** The planes intersect on a line.\n- **III.** The planes are coincident." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186338946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59fa73e3-7e11-58b8-85f9-3fcdbce086ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338946 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-3,1⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+2j+5k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,5⟩\\ne ⟨1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366212 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366212 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366212 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366212 #edb/ref 1071 "The intersection of the planes $3x+6y+3z=0$ and $x-y+2z+3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366212 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56b3e332-cb62-524a-a627-2ac9bac6fdaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366212 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\n3x+6y+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=-2y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z+3 & =0 \\\\ -2y-y+2(0)+3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =-3 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2y=-2$ and $a=⟨-2,1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x+6y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,6,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z+3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,6,3⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =15i-3j-9k \\\\  & =⟨15,-3,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨5,-1,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-2,1,0⟩+t⟨5,-1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2+1=-1,$ and $b=⟨5,-1,-3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366213 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe204930-21f0-5f11-87d8-144b4ea013e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366214 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨5,-1,3⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366214 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366214 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3221a9ad-33cd-5589-8d04-58da9238940e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366215 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0,\\,b=⟨3,-1,-5⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366215 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366215 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c700d7ec-2dc4-5c91-b31f-02251699ee00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366216 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨5,1,3⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366216 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366216 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50edec43-8dae-5c9f-ab3b-07f67a03c9d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366217 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=1,\\,b=⟨5,1,-3⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366217 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366217 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fe78330-aad4-5b51-9fc7-f31f9d446de9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366218 #edb/ref 1080 "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1,\\,b=⟨5,-1,-3⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366218 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366218 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "86b7c672-b3df-5dbe-8e30-f48eb2411bdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452601 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452602 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452603 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366219 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $x-2y+z=0$ and $x-y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- **II.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96c9a429-d310-55eb-845b-95fc86ca78d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366219 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388006 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3z-11 &=0 \\\\ 2x-2y-5 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186388006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0aedac8-4550-5f55-b4ac-09ba9ac8ed13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388006 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\times ⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j-4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186388010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388007 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "38007ff1-e837-5245-a985-db1b0cc61194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388008 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨-4,6,6⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388008 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388008 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d469811-ad49-51b7-ba98-f36dda7fa8bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388009 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨6,-4,6⟩" #edb/ref 1759218